      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Running shoes do not come with a use-by date, and the way most people judge them — by
          looking at the sole — is the least reliable way there is, especially for anyone who runs
          or walks indoors. The cushioning that matters is in the midsole foam, and foam wears out
          quietly. This page explains the mileage rule of thumb and where it comes from, converts
          it into months for your own routine, sets out why treadmill shoes are a special case,
          and lists the signs and habits that tell you when a pair has done its job.
        </p>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. How Often Should You Replace Running Shoes? The Short Answer</h2>
<p>
            <strong>The commonly cited rule of thumb is every 300 to 500 miles — for someone running
            15 miles a week, roughly every five to eight months.</strong> Treat that as a prompt to
            start checking, not a deadline: replace the pair when the cushioning feels flat or your
            legs start to complain on familiar sessions, whichever comes first.
          </p>
          <p>
            Two adjustments matter. Lighter, softer and racing-style shoes often reach the end of
            their useful life towards the lower end of the range, while durable daily trainers can
            go further; heavier runners tend to compress the foam faster than lighter ones. And on
            a treadmill, the visual check misleads: a belt is far kinder to rubber than asphalt, so
            the outsole of a treadmill-only pair often looks nearly new long after the midsole has
            lost its spring. Indoor runners should rely on mileage and feel rather than the tread.
          </p>
          <p>
            If you are replacing a pair now, the safest choice is usually the current version of
            the shoe you already like. If you are choosing afresh for indoor running, our
            <a href="/best-running-shoes-for-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">shortlist of running shoes that suit a treadmill belt</a>
            covers seven current models and what the belt changes about choosing one.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="mileage-rule" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. The 300 to 500 Mile Rule of Thumb</h2>
<p>
            The 300 to 500-mile figure is repeated by shoe shops, coaches and running publications
            so consistently that it can sound like a specification. It is not. It is a convention:
            a practical range that experience has settled on for when a typical pair of cushioned
            running shoes has lost enough of its cushioning and support that a replacement is worth
            considering. No shoe has a switch that flips at mile 400.
          </p>
          <p>
            The range is wide because the things that wear a shoe out vary widely. In general terms,
            and without putting numbers on any of them:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>The shoe.</strong> Lightweight trainers and racing shoes use thinner or
            softer foams and minimal outsole rubber to save weight, and are generally expected to
            wear sooner. Heavier, more durable daily trainers with more rubber are generally
            expected to last longer.</li>
            <li><strong>The runner.</strong> More body weight means more load through the foam on
            every step. A forceful footstrike, or a gait that loads one part of the shoe heavily,
            concentrates wear.</li>
            <li><strong>The surface.</strong> Rough asphalt and concrete grind the outsole away;
            trails scuff and tear uppers; a treadmill belt does neither, which is the subject of a
            section below.</li>
            <li><strong>The use.</strong> Easy running, fast running, walking and wearing the shoes
            to the shops all wear them differently. Shoes worn all day as casual shoes accumulate
            wear that never shows up in a training log.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            The useful way to use the rule is as a window. Below about 300 miles, assume a pair in
            normal use has plenty left unless something is obviously wrong. Between 300 and 500,
            start paying attention to how it feels. Beyond 500, keep running in it only if it still
            feels good and you are watching for the signs listed further down.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="miles-to-months" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. What 300 to 500 Miles Means in Months</h2>
<p>
            Most people do not think in shoe miles, so here is the rule converted into months. This
            is pure arithmetic: the mileage divided by your weekly distance, converted at about 4.3
            weeks to the month and rounded.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[480px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Miles per week</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Reaches 300 miles</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Reaches 500 miles</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">5</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">About 14 months</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">About 23 months</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">10</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">About 7 months</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">About 11.5 months</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">15</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">About 4.5 months</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">About 7.5 months</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">20</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">About 3.5 months</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">About 6 months</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">30</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">About 2.5 months</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">About 4 months</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">40</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Under 2 months</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">About 3 months</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            Two things are worth noticing. First, a recreational runner on 10 miles a week is
            looking at a pair every seven months to a year, which is longer than many people assume
            and a reason not to replace shoes on a calendar schedule. Second, a runner <a href="/treadmill-marathon-training/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>training for
            a marathon</a> at 30 to 40 miles a week can go through a pair in a season, which is the
            point at which rotating two pairs, covered below, starts to make sense.
          </p>
          <p>
            Your treadmill console will tell you your weekly distance if you do not already know
            it. If you need to convert between speed, time and distance, our reference on
            <a href="/how-long-is-a-mile-on-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how long a mile takes on a treadmill</a>
            has the tables.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="treadmill-wear" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. Why Treadmill Shoes Wear Differently</h2>
<p>
            A treadmill is not simply easier or harder on shoes than the road. It wears them
            differently, and the difference is exactly the one that fools the usual check.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The outsole lasts longer.</strong> A clean treadmill belt is smooth, flat and
            slightly cushioned. It does not grind rubber the way rough asphalt or concrete does, and
            there is no grit, gravel or kerb edge. The result is that the outsole on a treadmill-only
            pair can look almost new after hundreds of miles. On the road, a worn-through heel or
            forefoot is often the first visible prompt to replace a shoe; indoors, that prompt may
            never arrive.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The midsole still compresses.</strong> The foam between the outsole and your
            foot is what provides most of the cushioning you feel, and it is compressed on every
            step whatever you are running on. A treadmill deck flexes a little, but by a fraction
            of an inch, while the shoe carries well over an inch of foam under the heel; our guide
            to
            <a href="/treadmill-cushioning-explained/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill cushioning</a>
            explains why the shoe does most of the work. So the part of the shoe that wears out in
            the way that matters keeps wearing out at a broadly similar rate indoors, while the part
            you can see does not.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The same stride, thousands of times.</strong> Outdoors, cambers, corners, hills
            and changes of surface vary how each step lands. On a belt, every step lands in almost
            exactly the same way, at the same pace, on the same surface. That consistency is part of
            the treadmill's appeal, and it means the foam is compressed in the same pattern for the
            whole session. It is one more reason that a shoe's fit and feel matter on a treadmill.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Heat and sweat.</strong> Indoor running is warmer, feet sweat and swell more, and
            a pair that is rarely allowed to dry fully between sessions has a damp, tired feel
            sooner. That is about comfort and hygiene rather than cushioning, but it is part of why
            treadmill shoes can feel "done" before the miles say so.
          </p>
          <p>
            The practical conclusion is the one in the short answer: indoor runners should track
            miles and pay attention to feel, and treat a clean outsole as no evidence at all.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-wears" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. What Actually Wears Out in a Running Shoe</h2>
<p>
            A running shoe is several parts that age at different rates. Knowing which is which
            makes the signs below easier to read.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The midsole foam</strong> is the main event. It compresses under each step and
            springs back, and over many miles it springs back a little less. Running shoe foams
            differ widely — brands describe theirs in their own terms, and reviewers disagree about
            how soft a given shoe feels — but all of them eventually lose some of their rebound.
            A shoe whose foam has gone feels flatter, firmer or more "slappy" than it did, and the
            change is gradual enough that many runners do not notice until they try a new pair.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The outsole</strong> is the rubber that meets the ground. Outdoors it wears
            down, often unevenly, and on some shoes exposed foam patches wear faster still.
            Indoors it barely changes.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The heel counter and upper</strong> hold your foot over the midsole. A heel
            counter that has softened, an upper that has stretched or torn, or a lining worn
            through at the heel all reduce how well the shoe holds you, even if the cushioning is
            fine.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The insole</strong> is the removable layer inside. It compresses and flattens
            too, and some runners replace it separately, but a new insole does not restore a tired
            midsole.
          </p>
          <p>
            It is commonly said that foam needs time to recover between runs, and that running in
            the same pair every day wears it faster than alternating. That idea is widespread and
            plausible, and it is one of the arguments made for rotation, but treat it as a common
            view rather than an established fact. The rotation section below gives reasons to
            rotate that do not depend on it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="signs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. Signs Your Running Shoes Need Replacing</h2>
<p>
            Mileage tells you when to start looking. These tell you what you are looking for. Any
            one of them, in a pair that is already a few hundred miles in, is a good reason to
            replace it.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>The cushioning feels flat or firm</strong>, especially under the forefoot,
            compared with how it felt when new. If you are not sure, put on a new pair of the same
            model in a shop and walk around; the difference is often obvious.</li>
            <li><strong>Your legs feel unusually beaten up after familiar sessions.</strong> If a
            run you do every week starts leaving your legs heavier or achier than it used to, with
            nothing else changed, the shoes are one of the first things to look at. Persistent or
            sharp pain is a different matter and a reason to see a professional, not just to buy
            shoes.</li>
            <li><strong>Deep creases in the midsole foam</strong>, particularly horizontal wrinkles
            along the side of the heel, which show the foam has been compressed repeatedly.</li>
            <li><strong>The shoe leans when viewed from behind.</strong> Put the pair on a flat
            floor at eye level and look from the back. A shoe that tilts noticeably inwards or
            outwards, or whose heel has collapsed to one side, has worn unevenly.</li>
            <li><strong>The heel counter has gone soft</strong>, or the upper has torn or stretched
            so that your foot moves around more than it did.</li>
            <li><strong>The outsole is worn through</strong> to the foam in places. Common on road
            shoes, rare on treadmill-only pairs.</li>
            <li><strong>The pair has passed 500 miles</strong> and you cannot remember when it last
            felt good. That is not a reason on its own, but it is a reason to check the others.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            The press test sometimes recommended — pushing a thumb into the midsole to see how it
            feels — is not very informative, because foam that feels fine to a thumb can still be
            tired under a full stride. Feel during a run, and the comparison with a new pair, are
            better guides.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="tracking" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. How to Track Running Shoe Mileage</h2>
<p>
            Tracking is the single habit that makes the rule of thumb usable, and it costs nothing.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Note the start date.</strong> Write the date you first ran in a pair inside the
            tongue or on the insole with a marker. Even if you track nothing else, that date and a
            rough weekly mileage let you estimate the total with the table above.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Use the treadmill console or your training app.</strong> If all your running is
            on one treadmill, the console's total distance or session history is a ready-made shoe
            log; note the total on the day you start a new pair and subtract. Many <a href="/best-running-watches-for-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>running watches</a>
            and training apps let you assign a pair of shoes to each activity and keep a running
            total, and some prompt you when a pair passes a mileage you set. Set the prompt to 300
            miles, and treat it as the point to start checking rather than a replacement date.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Know how accurate the distance is.</strong> Treadmill distance is calculated
            from belt speed, and on many home machines that is inferred rather than measured, so it
            can be a few percent out. For shoe tracking that hardly matters. If you want more
            accurate indoor distance, a foot pod — one of the picks in our
            <a href="/best-treadmill-accessories/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill accessories roundup</a>
            — measures it from your stride, and our
            <a href="/treadmill-foot-pod-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill foot pod guide</a>
            explains how they work. Our page on
            <a href="/is-my-treadmill-speed-accurate/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">whether your treadmill speed is accurate</a>
            covers the console side.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Count walking miles too.</strong> If you walk in your running shoes — on the
            treadmill, at an under-desk pad or around town — those miles compress the foam as well,
            even if each step is gentler. They belong in the total.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="rotation" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Rotating Two Pairs: Is It Worth It?</h2>
<p>
            Rotating means alternating between two or more pairs rather than wearing one until it
            is finished. Many runners who train several times a week do it, and the case for it
            rests on a few practical points rather than on any guarantee.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>It costs less than it sounds.</strong> As simple arithmetic, two pairs used
            alternately each cover half your miles, so each lasts about twice as long in calendar
            time. Over a year you buy roughly the same number of shoes as you would wearing one
            pair at a time; you just buy the first two together.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>It gives you a reference.</strong> With one pair, foam fatigue creeps up so
            slowly that you do not notice. With two pairs of different ages, the older one's
            flatness is obvious the day after you run in the newer one. If you stagger the start
            dates by a couple of months, you always have a fresher pair to compare against.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>It lets each pair dry.</strong> Indoor running soaks shoes. A pair that has a
            day or two to dry between sessions is more pleasant to put on, and many runners believe
            it lets the foam recover too — the common view mentioned above.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>It suits different sessions.</strong> A cushioned daily trainer for easy runs and
            a lighter shoe for faster sessions is a common pairing, and our treadmill shoe
            roundup suggests exactly that combination. If you rotate between shoes with different
            drops or very different cushioning, introduce the new one gradually: every stride on a
            belt is the same, so a sudden change is repeated thousands of times. Our guide to
            <a href="/treadmill-running-form/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill running form</a>
            explains why changing too much at once is the commonest way to get hurt.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Keep one pair indoors.</strong> Shoes worn outside carry grit onto the belt, and
            grit is what wears belts and decks. A pair kept for the treadmill stays cleaner, and so
            does the machine.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="walking" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. Walkers, Desk Walkers and Incline: Does the Rule Change?</h2>
<p>
            The 300 to 500-mile convention grew up around running, and walking is gentler on a shoe
            per step. That does not mean <a href="/best-walking-shoes-for-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>walking shoes</a> last forever, because walking can rack up
            miles faster than people expect.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Desk walkers in particular.</strong> Two hours a day on an under-desk <a href="/best-walking-pads/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>walking
            pad</a> at 2 mph is 4 miles a day; across a five-day week that is 20 miles, or roughly 300
            miles in about 15 weeks. That is more weekly distance than many recreational runners
            cover. The impact per step is lower, so the foam may last longer per mile, but it is
            worth tracking and checking on the same principles. If you walk at a desk, a
            comfortable, cushioned shoe kept for the pad is a sensible habit, and our guide to
            <a href="/under-desk-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">under-desk treadmills</a>
            covers the rest of that set-up.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Incline walking.</strong> Walking at steep inclines changes how the foot loads
            the shoe, with more time on the forefoot and more work for the calves. It does not
            change the principle: track the miles, watch for the signs, and replace the pair when
            it no longer feels supportive. Some incline walkers prefer a highly cushioned shoe, and
            the treadmill shoe roundup includes one chosen with incline walking in mind.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Casual wear.</strong> The quickest way to wear out running shoes without running
            in them is to wear them all day. If you like a model for everyday wear, consider a
            separate pair, so your running pair's mileage means something.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="make-them-last" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. Making a Pair Last Without Cutting Corners</h2>
<p>
            Nothing makes foam immortal, but a few habits avoid wearing a pair out faster than
            the miles require.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Untie them to take them off.</strong> Levering a shoe off with the other
            foot breaks down the heel counter.</li>
            <li><strong>Dry them at room temperature.</strong> Take out the insoles, loosen the
            laces and let them air. Direct heat from radiators, heaters and tumble dryers is
            commonly cited as harmful to foams and adhesives; follow the brand's care advice.</li>
            <li><strong>Keep them for running.</strong> Every mile walked to the shops is a mile
            your running total does not know about.</li>
            <li><strong>Keep the belt clean.</strong> A clean treadmill belt is kinder to shoes and
            the shoes are kinder to the belt. Our guide to
            <a href="/how-to-clean-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">cleaning a treadmill</a>
            covers it.</li>
            <li><strong>Do not run in socks or bare feet</strong> to save your shoes. A moving belt
            and skin or cotton do not mix; our page on
            <a href="/can-you-walk-barefoot-on-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">walking barefoot on a treadmill</a>
            explains why.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            What not to do is stretch a pair well past the point where it feels flat to save money.
            A pair of shoes is cheap compared with the weeks you lose if your legs start to
            complain, and if they do, see a professional rather than relying on a new pair alone.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-to-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">11. If You Are Buying a Replacement Pair</h2>
<p>
            When the time comes, three rules cover most decisions.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you liked the old pair, buy its current version.</strong> That is almost
            always a better bet than switching to something new from a list. Most popular models
            change modestly from year to year; check reviews for anything that has changed a lot,
            such as the fit or the foam.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Change one thing at a time.</strong> A sudden switch in drop, cushioning or
            shape is repeated on every stride. If you do change, mix the old and new pairs for a
            few weeks while the old one still has some life in it.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>For treadmill running, weight fit and breathability above outsole
            toughness.</strong> The belt is flat, dry and kind to rubber, so grip and durable
            outsoles matter less than they do outside, while breathability matters more because
            treadmill rooms are warm. Leave a thumb's width at the toe for swelling, and try shoes
            on in the afternoon with your running socks.
          </p>
          <p>
            If you want a shortlist, our guide to the
            <a href="/best-running-shoes-for-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">top treadmill running shoes for 2026</a>
            covers cushioned daily trainers, a lighter tempo shoe, a stability shoe and a
            high-cushion pick for incline walkers, with what each gives up. And if you have pain,
            an injury or a foot condition, see a podiatrist or physical therapist before choosing,
            because the right shoe for you may not be on any list.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>


      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">12. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many miles do running shoes last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The commonly cited rule of thumb is 300 to 500 miles. It is a convention rather than a measured limit: lightweight and racing shoes often reach the end sooner, durable daily trainers can go further, and heavier runners tend to wear foam faster. Use the range as a prompt to start checking how the shoes feel, not as a fixed replacement date.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do treadmill running shoes last longer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The outsole does, because a treadmill belt wears rubber far more slowly than asphalt. The midsole foam, which provides most of the cushioning, still compresses on every step. So a treadmill-only pair can look nearly new while its cushioning is finished. Track the miles and judge by feel, not by the tread.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How often should I replace running shoes if I run 10 miles a week?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                By the 300 to 500-mile rule of thumb, roughly every seven months to a year. That is arithmetic: 300 miles at 10 a week is 30 weeks, and 500 is 50 weeks. Start checking how the shoes feel from around the seven-month mark, and replace them when the cushioning feels flat or your legs start to complain.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How can you tell if running shoes are worn out?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The best signs are feel-based: cushioning that feels flat or firm, and legs that feel unusually beaten up after familiar runs. Visible signs include deep creases in the midsole foam, a shoe that leans when viewed from behind on a flat floor, a softened heel counter and a torn upper. Comparing with a new pair often makes it obvious.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it worth rotating two pairs of running shoes?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For anyone running several times a week, usually yes. Two pairs used alternately each last about twice as long in calendar time, so the cost is similar. Rotation gives each pair time to dry, lets you compare an older pair with a fresher one, and lets you use different shoes for easy and faster sessions.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do walking miles count towards running shoe mileage?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Walking is gentler per step, but it still compresses the foam, and walking can add miles quickly: two hours a day at 2 mph on an under-desk pad is about 20 miles a week. Count walking miles in the same shoes, and keep a separate pair for casual wear so your running total stays meaningful.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes With Running Shoe Replacement</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Most of these come from judging shoes by how they look rather than how far they have gone and how they feel.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Judging a treadmill pair by its outsole</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A treadmill belt barely marks rubber, so an indoor pair can look new while its foam is finished. Note the date you started each pair, keep a mileage total from the console or your training app, and replace it when it feels flat, whatever the tread looks like.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating 500 miles as a hard limit</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">The 300 to 500-mile range is a convention. Some shoes feel finished well before 500 and some feel fine beyond it. Use the range to decide when to start checking, then let feel, creasing and lean decide. Replacing a pair that still feels good just because of a number wastes money.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Wearing running shoes all day</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Every mile walked around town compresses the same foam as your runs, but none of it appears in your training log. A pair that has also been your everyday shoe will be done sooner than its running mileage suggests. Keep running shoes for running, or keep a separate pair for casual wear.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Changing everything when you replace a pair</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Switching to a very different drop, cushioning or shape at the same time as retiring a worn pair means every stride changes at once. Buy the current version of a shoe you liked, or mix old and new pairs for a few weeks, and change one thing at a time.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

